%N A258205 Number of strictly non-overlapping holeless polyhexes of perimeter 2n with bilateral symmetry, counted up to rotation.
%C A258205 This sequence counts by perimeter length those holeless polyhexes that stay same when they are flipped over and rotated appropriately.
%C A258205 For n >= 1, a(n) gives the total number of terms k in A258005 with binary width = 2n + 1, or equally, with A000523(k) = 2n.
%C A258205 For even n the mirror axis of every such polyhex passes through two boundary vertices; mirror axes that bisect two boundary edges occur only for odd n. This is the source of the odd/even alternation of the terms. - _Tyler Satchel Orden_, Sep 06 2026
%H A258205 Tyler Satchel Orden, <a href="/A258205/a258205_2.txt">C++ program computing a(1)-a(19) (also A258204 and A258206)</a>
%H A258205 Tyler Satchel Orden, <a href="/A258205/a258205_3.txt">C++ program computing a(n) directly from the mirror-symmetric polygons (half-walk enumeration)</a>
%F A258205 Other identities and observations. For all n >= 1:
%F A258205 a(n) = 2*A258206(n) - A258204(n).
%F A258205 a(n) <= A258018(n).
%F A258205 a(n) = 2*A258206(n) - A006774(n)/6 for n == 2 or 4 (mod 6). (A polyhex with h cells and i internal edges has perimeter 2n = 6h - 2i; a 180-degree rotational symmetry forces n odd and a 120-degree one forces 3 | n, so for n == 2, 4 (mod 6) the only nontrivial symmetries are reflections and Burnside's lemma over the point group reduces to this.) - _Tyler Satchel Orden_, Sep 06 2026
%o A258205 (Scheme)
%o A258205 (define (A258205 n) (let loop ((k (+ 1 (expt 2 (+ n n)))) (c 0)) (cond ((pow2? k) c) (else (loop (+ 1 k) (+ c (if (isA258005? k) 1 0)))))))
%o A258205 (define (pow2? n) (let loop ((n n) (i 0)) (cond ((zero? n) #f) ((odd? n) (and (= 1 n) i)) (else (loop (/ n 2) (1+ i)))))) ;; Gives non-false only when n is a power of two.
